{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md | sed -n '1,260p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.repl link policy: what a pending require does when nobody answers\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: ADOPTED by owner ruling (2026-09-27): Option C of the\n     4\tpending-link failure policy -- the clock-free linker stands, the\n     5\t:link-policy seam ships (:manual default, function policies, :lease\n     6\treserved for phase 2), and the pending require returns a lease-aware\n     7\thandle map by default. Implementation pending. Subordinate to\n     8\t[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md) and to\n     9\t[`yin.vm.linker.md`](./yin.vm.linker.md); it settles the M5 decision that\n    10\t`yin.vm.linker.md` section 12 (bullet \"Failure policy\") left open. The\n    11\tmechanism it points at is [`dao.lease.md`](./dao.lease.md).\n    12\t\n    13\t## 1. The problem\n    14\t\n    15\tM5 wires `require` at the prompt to the linker. A require whose content\n    16\tsource does not answer stays `:pending`. `yin.repl` parks it as the\n    17\tshell's `:pending-run`: the prompt returns, lines typed meanwhile are\n    18\tretained and replayed once, in order, when the link completes, and\n    19\t`(abandon)` ends the wait and drops the retained lines. Nothing ends a\n    20\tpending run by itself.\n    21\t\n    22\tThat is right for a person at a prompt, who can type `(abandon)`. It is\n    23\twrong for a session nobody watches: a require against a source that never\n    24\tanswers sits pending forever, and the lines queued behind it never run.\n    25\tRetry cadence, deadline and permanent absence are the composition's\n    26\t(`yin.vm.linker.md` D6 and section 6.3), so the policy belongs to\n    27\t`yin.repl`, and the owner asked that it be configurable.\n    28\t\n    29\t## 2. Rulings that bound the design\n    30\t\n    31\t- `fetch` takes no deadline. The linker and the engine stay clock-free.\n    32\t  Time reaches a policy only as data the composition supplies.\n    33\t- `require` stays an ordinary function. A deadline is a session setting,\n    34\t  not an argument of `require` and not a new special form.\n    35\t- The M5 pending run, `(abandon)`, the retained-line replay and the\n    36\t  identity carry across rollbacks are unchanged. A policy adds one more\n    37\t  way to reach the abandon path; it adds no second path.\n    38\t- Rule R and the sealed private resources stand: a policy is composition\n    39\t  code, never a program value, and no program can reach its state.\n    40\t\n    41\t## 3. The option\n    42\t\n    43\t`yin.repl/create-state` takes `:link-policy`. It is a session setting kept\n    44\ton the shell beside `:link-source`, so `(reset)` and `(vm ...)` rebuild the\n    45\tsession without losing it.\n    46\t\n    47\t```text\n    48\t+-----------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n    49\t| Value     | Meaning                                                |\n    50\t+===========+========================================================+\n    51\t| :manual   | The default. Today's behavior: nothing ends a pending  |\n    52\t|           | run but `(abandon)`. No existing test changes.         |\n    53\t+-----------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n    54\t| a function| An embedder's rule, (fn [view]) returning :keep,       |\n    55\t|           | :abandon or {:abandon reason}. The REPL owns no clock; |\n    56\t|           | time reaches the function only through what it closes  |\n    57\t|           | over.                                                  |\n    58\t+-----------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n    59\t| :lease    | Reserved for the dao.lease deadline of section 6. In   |\n    60\t|           | phase 1 it is refused, \"not implemented yet\".          |\n    61\t+-----------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n    62\t```\n    63\t\n    64\tAny other value is refused at `create-state` with an `ex-info` naming the\n    65\tsupported values. The option fails closed at assembly, not at the first\n    66\tpending require.\n    67\t\n    68\t### 3.1 The view\n    69\t\n    70\tThe function receives plain data and nothing else:\n    71\t\n    72\t```text\n    73\t{:links [{:name 'foo :link-id [:t0 3]} ...]\n    74\t :checks n\n    75\t :lines-retained m}\n    76\t```\n    77\t\n    78\t`:links` are the requests still pending. `:checks` counts the re-checks of\n    79\tthis pending run that made no progress; a re-check that advanced a serve\n    80\tcursor, delivered a response or completed an install resets the count.\n    81\t`:lines-retained` is the number of typed lines waiting behind the run.\n    82\tThe view carries no clock reading, no handle and no secret.\n    83\t\n    84\t### 3.2 When the policy is consulted\n    85\t\n    86\t- when a require parks, with `:checks` 0;\n    87\t- after each re-check that leaves the run pending.\n    88\t\n    89\tIt is never consulted when nothing is pending, and never on a re-check\n    90\tthat completes the run: a completion is not a failure.\n    91\t\n    92\t### 3.3 What the answers do\n    93\t\n    94\t- `:keep` leaves the run as it is.\n    95\t- `:abandon`, or `{:abandon reason}`, runs the same abandon path as\n    96\t  `(abandon)`: installs in flight are refused, every link wait entry is\n    97\t  retired with the reason raised as the require's error, the shell returns\n    98\t  to the round the require began in, and the link identity minted by the\n    99\t  parked evaluation is carried onto the base. The reason defaults to\n   100\t  `:yin.repl/link-policy`; `(abandon)` keeps `:yin.repl/abandoned`.\n   101\t- The printed message says that the session policy ended the require, in\n   102\t  the same shape as the message `(abandon)` prints, so a policy abandon is\n   103\t  never mistaken for a user's.\n   104\t- Retained lines are dropped once and reported once, exactly as\n   105\t  `(abandon)` does. No line is evaluated twice, none is lost, and a late\n   106\t  response to an abandoned link is skipped and cannot settle a later\n   107\t  require (the M5 identity-carry guarantees hold unchanged).\n   108\t\n   109\t### 3.4 A policy that misbehaves\n   110\t\n   111\tA function that throws, or returns a value outside the contract, is\n   112\ttreated as `:keep` for that consult and surfaces as one shell error line.\n   113\tIt is never a silent abandon and never ends the session. A broken policy\n   114\tmust not be able to destroy the work it was meant to protect.\n   115\t\n   116\t### 3.5 State summary\n   117\t\n   118\t`repl-state`'s `:pending` gains the policy name (`:manual` or `:fn`) and\n   119\t`:checks`, so a host can display why a run is still waiting.\n   120\t\n   121\t## 4. Driving a re-check without a typed line\n   122\t\n   123\tToday a re-check happens when the user types a line. An unattended host\n   124\tcannot make a function policy act if nothing triggers a re-check. The host\n   125\tdrivers (`yin/repl/main.cljc`, `yin/repl/driver.cljc`) already own cadence,\n   126\tso the design question is only whether they can already ask the shell to\n   127\tre-check a pending run without an input line.\n   128\t\n   129\t- If they can, nothing is added; this section is documentation.\n   130\t- If they cannot, the shell gains one small public step function that\n   131\t  performs a re-check and consults the policy, and returns the new state\n   132\t  and the printed text. It reads no clock and takes no callback; the host\n   133\t  decides when to call it.\n   134\t\n   135\tWhich of the two holds is settled by reading those namespaces at\n   136\timplementation time. Either way the REPL itself stays free of clocks,\n   137\ttimers, callbacks and global atoms (the `yin.repl` namespace docstring).\n   138\t\n   139\t## 5. Why a function comes first\n   140\t\n   141\tA function policy covers every rule an embedder can want, from \"give up\n   142\tafter 20 re-checks\" to a host wall-clock budget the function closes over,\n   143\twith no new machinery in the REPL. It needs no tick source, no judge and no\n   144\tauthentication, because the policy runs in the same trust domain as the\n   145\tshell it configures. Phase 2 exists only for the case a function cannot\n   146\tserve: a deadline that must be a first-class, inspectable fact on a stream.\n   147\t\n   148\t## 6. Phase 2: `:lease`\n   149\t\n   150\tThis section is the design the reserved value stands for. It is optional and\n   151\tis built only when unattended use makes a data-visible deadline necessary.\n   152\t\n   153\t### 6.1 The mapping\n   154\t\n   155\t`dao.lease.md` defines a lease as a grant that lapses unless renewed. For a\n   156\tpending link:\n   157\t\n   158\t```text\n   159\t+-------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n   160\t| dao.lease         | In the REPL                                      |\n   161\t+===================+==================================================+\n   162\t| subject           | the pending link, [:link link-id]                |\n   163\t+-------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n   164\t| grantor and judge | the shell's link-policy judge, composed inside   |\n   165\t|                   | yin.repl, the boundary that possesses the link   |\n   166\t+-------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n   167\t| holder            | the shell's drive, the code that steps the link  |\n   168\t+-------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n   169\t| renewal           | the drive's own append when a step made progress |\n   170\t+-------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n   171\t| tick              | a host adapter's readings on a stream the shell  |\n   172\t|                   | wires: one for the judge, one for the holder     |\n   173\t+-------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n   174\t| reclaim procedure | the abandon of section 3.3, idempotent,          |\n   175\t|                   | reporting success                                |\n   176\t+-------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n   177\t| :lapsed :silence  | the trigger for that abandon                     |\n   178\t+-------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n   179\t```\n   180\t\n   181\tThe link is abandoned when the judge's interval since the last relevant\n   182\tobservation exceeds the granted duration plus tolerance (`:silence`).\n   183\t`:cap` also applies if the grant carries a maximum, giving a hard ceiling\n   184\thowever often the link progresses.\n   185\t\n   186\t### 6.2 What the option carries\n   187\t\n   188\t```text\n   189\t:link-policy {:lease {:duration {:seconds 30}\n   190\t                      :tolerance {:seconds 2}\n   191\t                      :max {:seconds 300}        ; optional cap\n   192\t                      :ticks tick-source}}       ; host adapter\n   193\t```\n   194\t\n   195\tUnits are the composition's interoperability decision, fixed once. The\n   196\tsizing relations of `dao.lease.md` hold: the duration exceeds twice the\n   197\trenewal interval, and the tolerance covers flight time and the rate skew\n   198\tbetween the judge's and the holder's tick streams.\n   199\t\n   200\t### 6.3 Attribution comes by construction\n   201\t\n   202\t`dao.lease.md` makes attribution a composition duty, and warns that\n   203\tin-process media distinguish no writers unless the composition arranges it.\n   204\tHere it can be arranged completely: the lease media and the tick streams\n   205\tare the shell's own, per-author, and never placed in a VM's private\n   206\t`:resources` or handed to any program value. A program holds only sealed\n   207\treferences to resources the engine issued, so it cannot append a forged\n   208\tgrant or renewal. The resolver is therefore the shell's own knowledge of\n   209\twhich medium each author writes. A capability token such as ShiBi is not\n   210\tneeded for this case and would add nothing until leases guard something\n   211\tthat untrusted writers can reach.\n   212\t\n   213\t### 6.4 What phase 2 costs\n   214\t\n   215\t- a tick adapter per host (a timer on the JVM, on Node and on Dart), which\n   216\t  is the one place a host clock is read, outside the REPL core;\n   217\t- a judge, driven at a declared cadence by the same host driver that\n   218\t  drives the shell;\n   219\t- lease bookkeeping that survives the rollbacks M5 fixed: the lease for a\n   220\t  link is reclaimed by the same abandon path, and a link identity is never\n   221\t  reused on the surviving pair;\n   222\t- scripted-tick tests, as `dao.lease`'s own tests are: no test reads a\n   223\t  real clock;\n   224\t- process-scoped leases only. The judge's ledger is not rebuildable from\n   225\t  any stream and the resource is not durable, so a restart drops the\n   226\t  session and its pending runs with it; `dao.lease.md`'s durable-resource\n   227\t  requirements do not apply.\n   228\t\n   229\tIt would be `dao.lease`'s first consumer, so it may surface gaps in that\n   230\tdesign; that is a reason to build it deliberately, not first.\n   231\t\n   232\t## 7. Open decisions for the owner\n   233\t\n   234\t1. Is a function policy enough until unattended use appears? This design\n   235\t   assumes yes.\n   236\t2. If a lease deadline is wanted, is the pending link the right subject,\n   237\t   or should the lease cover the whole pending run (all its links and the\n   238\t   retained lines)? A per-run lease is simpler and makes one abandon; a\n   239\t   per-link lease lets one stuck link lapse while another progresses.\n   240\t3. Whether the message for a policy abandon should name the policy value\n   241\t   that fired (`:fn`, `:lease :silence`, `:lease :cap`).\n   242\t\n   243\t## 8. Tests the implementation must pin\n   244\t\n   245\tPhase 1, portable to JVM, Node and Dart, comparing values and structured\n   246\tstate, not printed text (ClojureDart prints some forms differently):\n   247\t\n   248\t- `:manual` is the default and a pending run survives any number of\n   249\t  re-checks;\n   250\t- a function returning `:abandon` at a chosen `:checks` ends the require\n   251\t  with the policy reason and one message; `:keep` never ends it;\n   252\t- a function that throws, or returns a value outside the contract, is\n   253\t  kept and reported;\n   254\t- an unknown option value and `:lease` are refused at `create-state`;\n   255\t- the policy survives `(reset)` and `(vm ...)`, and `:checks` resets;\n   256\t- retained lines are dropped once on a policy abandon, and a retained line\n   257\t  that starts a second pending require still behaves as M5 requires;\n   258\t- a late response after a policy abandon cannot settle a later require.\n   259\t\n   260\tPhase 2 adds scripted-tick tests: silence past duration plus tolerance\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
